{
  "dom_id": "step-q344896",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039683/assets/847d1ae3ff7602ae8793ec356dd3034650d8ba85adcb3be579097e46728667a0.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{\u2032}(3)$ where $p(x)=f(x)g(x).$\n\n${p}^{\u2032}(3)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(x)={f}^{\u2032}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{\u2032}(3)={f}^{\u2032}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $g(3)=1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{\u2032}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (2,4)$ equals $2.$ So, ${g}^{\u2032}(3)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(3) & ={f}^{\u2032}(3)\\cdot g(3)+f(3)\\cdot {g}^{\u2032}(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
